🧠 Introduction

The Java Collections Framework (JCF) is a set of classes and interfaces used to store and manipulate groups of data efficiently.

👉 Collections provide:

✔ Dynamic storage
✔ Efficient data management
✔ Built-in algorithms
✔ Easy data retrieval

---

🎯 Why Collections?

Arrays have limitations:

❌ Fixed size

❌ Difficult insertion and deletion

❌ Limited built-in methods

Collections solve these problems.

---

📌 Example

```java id="c1"
import java.util.ArrayList;

ArrayList<String> names =
        new ArrayList<>();

names.add("Java");
names.add("Python");
```

---

🏗️ Collection Framework Hierarchy

```text id="c2"
Iterable
    │
Collection
    │
├── List
├── Set
└── Queue

Map (Separate Interface)
```

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. Collection Interface

Root interface of the Collection Framework.

Common methods:

```java id="c3"
add()
remove()
contains()
size()
clear()
isEmpty()
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 2. List Interface

Stores ordered elements.

✔ Maintains insertion order
✔ Allows duplicates

---

📌 Implementations

* ArrayList
* LinkedList
* Vector
* Stack

---

📌 Example

```java id="c4"
List<String> list =
        new ArrayList<>();

list.add("Java");
list.add("Java");
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 3. Set Interface

Stores unique elements.

✔ No duplicates allowed

---

📌 Implementations

* HashSet
* LinkedHashSet
* TreeSet

---

📌 Example

```java id="c5"
Set<Integer> set =
        new HashSet<>();

set.add(10);
set.add(10);
```

Output:

```text id="c6"
10
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 4. Queue Interface

Used for processing elements in sequence.

✔ FIFO (First In First Out)

---

📌 Implementations

* PriorityQueue
* LinkedList
* ArrayDeque

---

📌 Example

```java id="c7"
Queue<Integer> queue =
        new LinkedList<>();

queue.add(10);
queue.add(20);

System.out.println(queue.poll());
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 5. Map Interface

Stores key-value pairs.

👉 Not part of Collection interface hierarchy.

---

📌 Implementations

* HashMap
* LinkedHashMap
* TreeMap
* Hashtable

---

📌 Example

```java id="c8"
Map<Integer, String> map =
        new HashMap<>();

map.put(1, "Java");
map.put(2, "Python");
```

---

# 📊 Collections Overview

| Interface | Ordered   | Duplicates  | Example    |
| --------- | --------- | ----------- | ---------- |
| List      | ✔ Yes     | ✔ Yes       | ArrayList  |
| Set       | Depends   | ❌ No        | HashSet    |
| Queue     | ✔ Yes     | ✔ Yes       | LinkedList |
| Map       | Key-Value | Keys Unique | HashMap    |

